WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.107
Now we are moving to generators.

00:00:02.107 --> 00:00:07.207
Generators are important because they allow Python to produce values one at a time.

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.717
Let's compare that with a regular list.

00:00:09.717 --> 00:00:13.827
If we create a list containing one million transformed values,

00:00:13.827 --> 00:00:18.662
Python normally creates and stores all one million values in memory.

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:20.887
A generator can work differently.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:26.117
It can calculate a value when we need it, return that value, and then continue later.

00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:29.227
The key word that makes this possible is yield.

00:00:29.227 --> 00:00:31.477
Look at the squares_gen function.

00:00:31.477 --> 00:00:33.892
We loop through the numbers up to n.

00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:37.107
Instead of using return, we use yield.

00:00:37.107 --> 00:00:41.942
When Python reaches yield, it gives us one value and pauses the function.

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:49.047
The next time Python asks the generator for another value, the function continues from where it stopped.

00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:52.157
Run the example. You will see the squared values printed one at a time.

00:00:52.157 --> 00:00:56.602
For four values, memory is obviously not a major concern.

00:00:56.602 --> 00:01:01.497
But imagine processing millions of records or reading a very large file.

00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:07.472
Generators can be useful because we do not always need to store every result at the same time.

00:01:07.472 --> 00:01:12.477
The important idea for now is that return usually finishes a function,

00:01:12.477 --> 00:01:16.640
while yield produces a value and allows the function to continue later.
